  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through seven innings until Rengifo broke it in the eighth.",
    "what_this_means": "Late-inning rallies have become the thread holding this season together, and last night confirmed it yet again. The Padres didn't need to lead early. They just needed the game to stay within reach by the time the lineup came alive. Rengifo's go-ahead homer in the eighth proved that formula still works, but it works because the bullpen keeps the door open. Kyle Hart, Wandy Peralta, Bradgley Rodriguez, Adrian Morejon, and Mason Miller combined for 5.2 innings of near-shutout relief after Germ\u00e1n M\u00e1rquez exited after 2.1. The offense converted 3 of 11 RISP situations into runs. That's not plenty, but it was just enough. This is a team built on fragile equilibrium. The starting pitcher holds it long enough, the bullpen preserves it, and if the bats show up late, the math works. Win by one run again. That math gets harder every time.",
    "what_to_watch": "Miami's Eury P\u00e9rez brings a 3.53 ERA into tonight's matchup. JP Sears takes the ball for the Padres at 1:10 PM PT.",
